#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114989 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "239ee94c-c3d9-5955-8cdd-afff77ec3279" :question/math-academy-id "e-4716" :question/problem "Is\n$E = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nan elementary matrix?" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation.\n\nThe given matrix $E$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I_2$ by applying the elementary row operation\n\n$$\nR_2:= R_2+(-5) R_1\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI_{2} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + (-5)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= E\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114990 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c56b3e9-cf23-5e1e-8c7d-c474059c9a9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "40cdd39f-29c3-5dad-9708-02c3e0de6acb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4718" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5}\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $E$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}E & I\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=\\frac{5}{2}R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114991 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd12bc7b-1b5d-5af2-bcef-c9d0fda7d8fc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Product Involving the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "ed71f469-f0e9-5016-846d-cb70d59ab3ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-4719" :question/problem "Find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1}$, where $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $E_1$ and $E_2$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find $E_2^{-1}$. We write down $E_2$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}E_{2} & I\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{1}:=R_{1} + 2R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, we can find the product $E_1 E_{2}^{-1}$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"}]}
